# Cache rules for Netlify / Cloudflare Pages.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT IN EFFECT ON THE CURRENT HOST. This site is served by GitHub Pages,
# which has no mechanism for setting response headers and ignores this file
# entirely. It sends a flat `Cache-Control: max-age=600` on everything.
#
# The consequence, measured rather than assumed: for those ten minutes the
# browser answers a /data/ request out of its own HTTP cache WITHOUT CONSULTING
# THE SERVICE WORKER AT ALL -- resource timing reports workerStart 0 and
# deliveryType "cache". So no amount of cleverness in sw.js can shorten that
# window; the worker is not running during it. An attempt to force the issue
# there with `cache: 'no-cache'` was written, tested against a local server
# reproducing these exact headers, found to change nothing, and removed.
#
# What actually happens: once the entry goes stale the worker is consulted
# again, its stale-while-revalidate path sees the new ETag, refreshes the
# corpus and prompts the reader. Verified end to end. So the practical cost is
# a ten-minute delay for someone who reloads immediately after a deploy, and
# none at all for a reader returning later -- their entry is long expired.
# Mostly it confuses whoever just deployed. To check a deploy landed, request
# a cache-busted URL (`/index.html?cb=123`); the plain URL may answer from
# cache and look like a deploy that never happened.
#
# Removing the window for real is a hosting change, not a code change: move to
# Netlify or Cloudflare Pages, both of which read this file, or put Cloudflare
# in front of Pages and set the rule there.
#
# Keep this file. It is correct, and it starts working the day the site moves
# to a host that reads it. Just do not trust it to be doing anything today.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The four data files are large and change rarely, but they MUST NOT be cached
# blindly -- when a promise is added or a verse corrected, readers have to get
# it. So they revalidate on every visit, and the host answers an unchanged file
# with a 304 (a few hundred bytes) instead of resending megabytes. That keeps
# updates instant without paying the download twice.

/data/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
